winemac: OnMainThread runs the block directly when called on the main thread.

-[WineContentView viewWillDraw] runs on the main thread during a Core Animation commit and calls
macdrv_update_opengl_context() for each pending GL context. When the context needs an update and has
no latent view, that calls OnMainThread(^{ [context update]; }). On the main thread there is no
WineEventQueue, so OnMainThread queued the block with OnMainThreadAsync() and waited on a semaphore
that only the main thread itself could signal: the main thread hung, and every Wine thread that then
waited for it hung too (a window being destroyed in macdrv_dispose_view, wglMakeCurrent in
macdrv_make_context_current, a new window in macdrv_get_input_source_info).

Seen with a WPF program on .NET Framework 4.8 (hardware rendering through d3d9, wined3d's OpenGL):
hovering a control opens a Popup (a GL-rendered layered window); the program froze at the next
DestroyWindow of that popup. Wine master (2026-11) still has the same code.

diff --git a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_event.m b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_event.m
index 5c4009f..298d363 100644
--- a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_event.m
+++ b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_event.m
@@ -489,6 +489,14 @@ void OnMainThread(dispatch_block_t block)
 {
 @autoreleasepool
 {
+    /* Already on the main thread (-[WineContentView viewWillDraw] updating a GL context): queueing the
+     * block and waiting for it would wait for this thread itself. */
+    if ([NSThread isMainThread])
+    {
+        block();
+        return;
+    }
+
     NSMutableDictionary* threadDict = [[NSThread currentThread] threadDictionary];
     WineEventQueue* queue = threadDict[WineEventQueueThreadDictionaryKey];
     dispatch_semaphore_t semaphore = NULL;
